# 90ms+90ms Confirmation & Spring Physics

Click feedback must reconcile spatial certainty with high-speed execution. TuffEx formalizes the confirmation rhythm and continuous spring dynamics.

## Why Instant Menu Dismissal Harms Usability

In many native operating systems, selecting an item from a context menu closes the panel within 0ms.
Users frequently freeze for a fraction of a second: "Did I actually click that option, or did my pointer slip outside?"
Missing feedback breaks the cognitive feedback loop.

## The Two-Beat Confirmation Rhythm (90ms + 90ms)

TuffEx items (`TxDropdownItem`, `TxContextMenuItem`) employ an immutable **180ms two-beat confirmation rhythm**:

```
[User triggers menu item selection]
  │
  ├─ [Beat 1: 90ms] Clears hover/focus highlights to dissolve pointer residue.
  │
  ├─ [Beat 2: 90ms] Pulses the active token fill to register visual confirmation.
  │
  └─ [Resolution] Emits the select event and closes the parent menu panel.
```

The entire cycle executes in 180ms, delivering confirmation without lagging the user. Under reduced motion, it degrades to 0ms instant execution.

## Cubic Bezier Limitations Under Gesture Interruption

Standard CSS cubic bezier transitions are **fixed-duration time interpolation curves**.
When user gestures interrupt an ongoing animation (such as catching a drawer mid-flight and reversing direction):
- Bezier curves cannot absorb initial velocity $\vec{v}_0$; they forcibly reset velocity to zero and restart.
- The visual motion stutters mechanically, violating physical intuition.

## The TuffEx Second-Order Damped Spring Engine

TuffEx motion models rely on the second-order damped harmonic oscillator equation:
$$m \frac{d^2x}{dt^2} + c \frac{dx}{dt} + k x = 0$$
- **Stiffness ($k$)**: Governs restorative spring force.
- **Damping ($c$)**: Dissipates oscillation energy, eliminating unwanted wobble.
- **Mass ($m$)**: Imparts physical inertia.

When interrupted, the spring system inherits the instantaneous velocity vector naturally, maintaining physical continuity.
